Bollywood actress Disha Patani’s sister Khushboo Patani, known for her fitness and frank comments on social media, has once again made headlines.
Raising concerns about the rising crimes against women and the country's security system, Khushboo has offered girls some strong advice.
In a widely circulated video, Khushboo Patani is seen urging girls to keep a 'pistol', that is, an air gun, for self-defence and to receive training in using it. She also suggested that girls should stop relying on others for their safety, considering the current situation of women in India.
Also Read: Ramayana promotional event delayed by four hours, Ranbir Kapoor tells media 'chale jao'; producer Namit Malhotra later apologises 
Khushboo Patni took to her Instagram and shared a video in which she can be seen holding an air gun that resembles a Glock pistol.
Sharing the video, Khushboo wrote in the caption, “Disclaimer: Only for self-defence. This is not a toy , no matter it is real or not real u must use it wisely and responsibly.177 Air Pistol Glock 17 Gen 4 For educational purpose only.”
She spoke to the camera, explaining to girls that they can respect themselves.
 View this post on Instagram
 A post shared by Major Khushboo Patani (@khushboo_patani) 
Khushboo Patni said in the video, "Girls, this is a copy of the Glock. It's a CO2 air pistol, which doesn't require a license in India. You just need to go to a good shooting range near you and practice it thoroughly. Learn to use the pistol. It's not very heavy, about 1.5-2 kg."
In the video, Khushboo removes the magazine from the pistol and explains that it does not contain real bullets, but rather small pellets (BBs), which are discharged at a very high speed and are sufficient to injure or frighten an attacker in self-defence.
She further explained, "You can protect yourself. There's no need to depend on people. Otherwise, what's happening in India will never stop! The pellets are inside, the target is in front.”
Khushboo Patni delivered a strong message to girls, voicing her concern about the current situation in the country.
